\description{
Read an ANDI-MS netCDF chromatogram, estimate a smoothly time-varying point
spread function, recover log-concave compounds with \code{\link[=splogconNMF]{splogconNMF()}}, and
write a NIST-compatible MSP spectral library. All original mass channels
are retained in the final spectra.
}
\details{
\strong{File formats.} ANDI-MS variables \code{scan_index}, \code{point_count},
\code{scan_acquisition_time}, \code{mass_values} and \code{intensity_values}
define the scan spectra. Scan indices are zero-based. Padding between scans
is accepted. Intensities must be finite and nonnegative. Times are converted
to minutes; absent units use the ANDI-MS seconds convention. The selected
retention-time interval is read directly from the point arrays.

MSP entries are ordered by profile apex time, with a unique compound name,
retention time and integrated ion intensity in the comments, followed by
mass/intensity pairs. Exported spectra have base peak 999 and retain every
positive fitted ion. \code{coef()} returns unnormalized fitted spectra.
With \code{normalize="l1"}, \code{coef()} returns spectra summing to one.
The MSP and R object retain every positive fitted ion. The spreadsheet's
compact spectrum text is a rounded display alongside the integrated areas.
Their compound profiles are \eqn{P_{ir}=H_{ir}\sum_j S_{rj}}, preserving
\eqn{HS=PT}. The workbook reports \code{area}, equal to \eqn{\sum_j S_{rj}}, in
intensity-scans, and \code{area_min}, the trapezoidal integral of
\eqn{P_{\cdot r}} over retention time, in intensity-minutes.

The returned object stores the factors and source CDF path. Observed data
are loaded from the CDF only when a plot needs them. Fitted signals are
reconstructed from the factors; the observed matrix is released after fitting.

\strong{PSF and background.} \code{\link[=estimate_psf]{estimate_psf()}} estimates constant or smoothly
time-varying PSFs from the sample, optionally starting from an alkane standard.
Original channels, outcome groups and the total-ion signal are supported.
Interpolated columns are projected to log concavity on their local support
and normalized to unit sum. Their typical full width at half maximum,
\eqn{w}, sets the background dimension to
\deqn{q=\max\{4,\min[40,3+\lceil n/(200w)\rceil]\}.}
Background coefficients are nonnegative and unpenalized. This keeps the
baseline much smoother than an individual elution peak.
The calibrated median PSF width also sets the guard for the final
broad-profile fusion pass in \code{\link[=splogconNMF]{splogconNMF()}}. Set
\code{control=list(profile_fusion=FALSE)} to disable that pass.

\strong{Shared noise model.} Noise parameters are estimated once from the
original channels and reused in PSF estimation and factorization. The PSF
weights include the information adjustment \eqn{1/\kappa}, where
\eqn{\kappa=1+2\widehat\rho_1}. Aggregation adjusts the variance parameters
according to the selected family; NB2 and ordinary Gamma use moment matching.
See \code{\link[=estimate_psf]{estimate_psf()}} for the distributional details. The returned
\code{psf_working_weight} summarises the average Poisson-scale precision for
comparing noise scales; the actual PSF likelihood uses the specified family.

\code{precluster=32L} performs selection and fusion in at most 32 size-adjusted
outcome groups, followed by refinement on every original channel. See
\code{\link[=splogconNMF]{splogconNMF()}} for the selection criterion and shape constraints. The
spreadsheet and returned coefficient rows use increasing apex retention time. Rank selection,
hard log concavity, censored Gamma intensities and compound-count information
criteria follow \code{\link[=splogconNMF]{splogconNMF()}}.
}
\section{Experimental GC/MS examples}{

The \href{../articles/gcms-deconvolution.html}{GC/MS deconvolution tutorial}
converts the bundled \link{GCMS_data1} and \link{GCMS_data2} matrices to CDF, fits the
full-channel models and exports their spectra. It includes timings,
information criteria and all plot types, alongside the algorithm.
